From 61e30feaa50e99aa521b93925c2503d274921dc1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Crindo18 <chrisjohnalado@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 08:55:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] M7 tooling: paired comparison, ablation variants, holdout
 duplicate check

The test-split runs are in and the enhanced system is 1.46 pp WORSE on
average across the -5..-20 dB band it targets (13 of 27 cells worse, 5
better; overall in-DB 69.8% baseline vs 69.1% enhanced). Success
criterion 1 is not met anywhere on the grid.

The regression is 3-4x larger at 5 s and 10 s, which is exactly where
Enhancement 2 is switched off - so those queries differ from baseline
only by denoising, adaptive picking, and an index with 2.3x the
postings. The wider enrolment zone applies to every query but only pays
back under 5 s, and more postings means more chance collisions raising
the offset-histogram floor.

Added to settle it:
  systemConfig 'enh1'  Enhancement 1 against the BASELINE enrolment
                       zone - isolates denoise + adaptive picking.
  systemConfig 'enh2'  Enhancement 2 only. Its 5 s / 10 s rows have a
                       query side identical to the baseline, so they
                       measure the wider index on its own.
  s08_compareSystems   exact McNemar per (length, SNR) cell, aligned by
                       queryID, with an explicit check that a file's
                       label matches the config tag it carries.
  s02b_checkHoldout    s05 had to push tau to 0.478 to hold FAR under
                       1%, but a genuine query scores ~0.28 clean - a
                       holdout above 0.4 is the same recording, not a
                       coincidence. Catalogues built from personal
                       libraries duplicate tracks across folders, and
                       each duplicate inflates FAR until tau rises far
                       enough to reject real queries too. That is the
                       observed signature: precision 1.000, FAR 0.000,
                       recall 0.04.
